\section{Experimental design}
\label{sec:experiments}

\paragraph{Data and sampling.}
We use the nine datasets of the \texttt{ppi-python} package (\texttt{galaxies}, \texttt{forest}, \texttt{alphafold}, \texttt{gene\_expression}, \texttt{census\_income}, \texttt{census\_healthcare}, \texttt{census\_education}, \texttt{plankton}, \texttt{ballots}) and a synthetic suite with known truth. The target is $\theta=\E[Y]$. On a real dataset the pool of labeled records is treated as the population, so the truth is its mean; each repetition draws $n$ labeled and $N$ unlabeled records independently with replacement, so records are i.i.d.\ and labels MCAR by construction. Two datasets need a different truth. \texttt{ballots} has no outcome on its unlabeled split, so it supports width comparisons by resampling the labeled records only, not a coverage claim on the real split. \texttt{plankton} has a distribution shift between its two years; the mean of the later year is known, and this is the cell where the i.i.d.\ assumption fails on purpose. The synthetic suite covers an exactly affine $\E[Y\mid f]$ (where \cref{cor:noroom} says there is no room), Gaussian designs with closed-form $\Delta$, sigmoid and non-monotone calibrations, binary $Y$ at prevalences down to 1\%, and informative side information.

\paragraph{Arms.}
All arms see the same draws. With $f$ only (T1): the labels-only interval; PPI and PPI++ from \texttt{ppi-python}~0.2.3, the latter being the baseline; PPI++ run on the calibrated score $\hat g(f)$; the cross-fitted estimator of \cref{sec:method}; the same estimator with $g$ chosen from \{PPI++, isotonic or spline calibration, gradient boosting\} by cross-validation inside each repetition, on folds separate from those used for the variance estimate; and the estimated floor $\hat V^\ast$ from a cross-fitted fit on the whole pool, which is not a valid interval but a ceiling on the headroom. With $f$ and $X$ (T2, census tasks and AlphaFold): vector PPI++ and the OLS-adjusted mean (the strongest linear baseline), gradient-boosting AIPW, the calibrated estimator with $X$, the floor with $X$, and the selection procedure among the calibrated and linear arms, again inside each repetition. The headline arm, fixed before the runs, is the selection procedure of each tier.

\paragraph{Measures and decision rule.}
The headline measure is the paired width ratio $r=\text{width(arm)}/\text{width(PPI++)}$ on the same draws, with a 95\% interval over repetitions. Since width scales as $n^{-1/2}$, $1-r^2$ is the fraction of labels saved. The rule was fixed before any run: a cell is \emph{no gain} if the interval for $r$ lies in $[0.98,1.02]$, a \emph{gain} if its upper bound is below $0.95$ and the arm is valid, and undecided otherwise. An arm is valid in a cell if its coverage is not significantly below nominal (Wilson interval) and not below that of PPI++ beyond Monte Carlo error; an invalid arm's width is reported but not counted as a gain. Headline cells use at least 2000 repetitions and small-$n$ or rare-event cells at least 5000. The sample sizes are log-spaced in $\{30,\dots,10^4\}$ as each pool allows, with a reference $n$ per dataset, and $N$ is the largest the pool allows under a stated cap, with $N/n\in\{1,4\}$ on three datasets to check the PPI to PPI++ step where $N\approx n$.
